import{_ as t,o as s,c as o,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.7R1OEGYz.js";const c=JSON.parse('{"title":"Usage and cost","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"usage.md","filePath":"usage.md","lastUpdated":1794056215000}'),a={name:"usage.md"};function r(i,e,d,h,l,u){return s(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="usage-and-cost" tabindex="-1">Usage and cost <a class="header-anchor" href="#usage-and-cost" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Usage and cost&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The <strong>Usage &amp; cost</strong> page shows how many tokens each of your sessions used and what the API-key sessions cost, plus the CPU time, memory and network traffic of their pods. You can set yourself a monthly API budget there. With Enterprise, admins can set budgets for the whole organisation, a group or a single user.</p><p><strong>Edition:</strong> Community (Usage page, personal budget) · Enterprise (organisation-wide limits) · <strong>Who:</strong> every signed-in user for their own sessions; admin for organisation limits</p><h2 id="opening-the-page" tabindex="-1">Opening the page <a class="header-anchor" href="#opening-the-page" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Opening the page&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Click <strong>Usage &amp; cost</strong> in the sidebar. The home page also shows <strong>Top spend by session</strong>, with a link to the full page. You only ever see your own sessions.</p><h2 id="what-the-page-shows" tabindex="-1">What the page shows <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-page-shows" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the page shows&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Card</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>API cost</strong></td><td>Money for API-key sessions, as the agent CLI itself reported it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Subscription usage</strong></td><td>Tokens (and turns, where the CLI records them) of sessions that run on a subscription login. No dollar amount: this counts against your plan&#39;s limits, not money.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tokens</strong></td><td>All tokens: input, output and cache.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Compute</strong></td><td>CPU time of your session pods, and network traffic received and sent.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Below that, <strong>Token mix</strong> splits all tokens into input, output, cache read and cache create.</p><p><strong>Cost by session</strong> lists every session with its tokens, token mix, pod resources (CPU time, peak memory, data received and sent) and a cost or usage column. The pill in that column says how the session is billed:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Pill</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Counts against the budget</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>api</code></td><td>Billed to your stored API key. Shows the reported cost, or <strong>not reported</strong> if the CLI reports tokens only.</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>sub</code></td><td>Covered by a subscription login. Shows turns.</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>self-hosted</code></td><td>A <a href="/docs/sessions">custom model endpoint</a>; no provider bill.</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>auto</code></td><td><strong>billing unknown</strong>: an interactive Claude session with both a login and an API key stored, where the CLI asked which to use.</td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Whether a session counts as API spend is decided by the credential the hub gave the session, not by the cost figure the CLI sends. Claude Code and OpenCode also report a price-table cost for subscription sessions; that figure does not count as spend.</p><h2 id="which-agents-are-recorded" tabindex="-1">Which agents are recorded <a class="header-anchor" href="#which-agents-are-recorded" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Which agents are recorded&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Tokens</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Claude Code</td><td>Yes, needs <code>telemetry.enabled</code> (default on)</td><td>Reported by the CLI</td></tr><tr><td>Codex</td><td>Yes</td><td>Not reported: the CLI computes no price</td></tr><tr><td>OpenCode</td><td>Yes</td><td>Reported by the CLI</td></tr><tr><td>OpenClaw</td><td>Yes</td><td>Reported by the CLI</td></tr><tr><td>Cursor</td><td><strong>Not recorded</strong></td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Codex, OpenCode and OpenClaw usage is read from each CLI&#39;s own records in the pod about every 30 seconds and when the agent exits. Only the numbers leave the pod, never conversation content. OpenCode does not count its own title-generation request, so its figures can be slightly below what the provider bills.</p><p>For Claude Code, leave <code>telemetry.otlpEndpoint</code> empty to send metrics to AgentHub. If an administrator selects an external collector, those exports do not count in AgentHub. See <a href="/docs/chart-values#telemetry-collectors">telemetry collectors</a> for the deployment setting and limits. After updating the server and runtime images, restart existing Claude sessions to receive the telemetry environment needed to keep recording usage.</p><p>If the table says <strong>No usage recorded yet</strong>, check that <code>telemetry.enabled</code> is set on the server for Claude sessions.</p><h2 id="your-monthly-api-budget" tabindex="-1">Your monthly API budget <a class="header-anchor" href="#your-monthly-api-budget" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Your monthly API budget&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Under <strong>Monthly API budget</strong>, enter an amount in <strong>My limit (USD/month)</strong> and click <strong>Save</strong>. Leave the field empty and save to remove your limit. The bar shows this calendar month&#39;s API spend (UTC) against the limit that applies to you. When an admin limit is stricter than yours, the bar says <strong>set by your admin</strong> and names its scope.</p><h3 id="what-happens-at-the-limit" tabindex="-1">What happens at the limit <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-happens-at-the-limit" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What happens at the limit&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Once this month&#39;s API spend reaches the limit, <strong>new API-billed sessions are blocked</strong>; subscription sessions still work. In detail:</p><ul><li>Starting a session that would bill an API key is refused with <em>&quot;Monthly API budget reached … Use a subscription session or raise the limit.&quot;</em> This applies when a session is created, resumed, restarted for a runtime update (<strong>Update now</strong>) or restarted to apply new resources, including sessions started from chat, webhooks or the API.</li><li>Sessions that are already running are not stopped.</li><li>Only agents whose CLI reports a cost are gated: Claude Code, OpenCode and OpenClaw. Codex and Cursor sessions are never refused, because their API use never adds to the budget.</li><li>Subscription sessions, self-hosted endpoints and <code>auto</code> sessions with unknown billing are never refused. An unattended Claude session in Auto mode with an API key stored counts as API-billed, because the CLI then uses the key.</li></ul><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">A budget, not a spending cap</p><p>The budget counts the API cost that the agents report. It is not a guarantee on your total provider spend: running sessions continue, and Codex and Cursor costs are not counted.</p></div><h2 id="organisation-wide-limits" tabindex="-1">Organisation-wide limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#organisation-wide-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Organisation-wide limits&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With an Enterprise licence, admins set limits under <strong>Settings → Usage limits &amp; groups</strong>:</p><ul><li><strong>Global limit</strong>: an amount per user and month that applies to everyone.</li><li><strong>Per-user and per-group limits</strong>: choose <strong>user</strong> or <strong>group</strong>, enter the username or group name and the amount, then click <strong>Add</strong>. Groups come from your OIDC provider&#39;s groups claim (see <a href="/docs/admin#groups-from-the-oidc-token">Administration</a>).</li></ul><p>For each user, the <strong>strictest</strong> limit wins: the lowest of the global limit, the limits of their groups, their own user limit and their personal limit. Each limit applies to that user&#39;s own spend; a group limit is not a shared pool for the whole group. Without a valid licence, only personal limits apply.</p><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/docs/chart-values">Chart values</a>: <code>telemetry.enabled</code></li><li><a href="/docs/admin">Administration</a></li></ul>',28)])])}const g=t(a,[["render",r]]);export{c as __pageData,g as default};
